.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Perrin (1926) related the polarization of fluorescence to how far a molecule rotates during its excited-state lifetime \tau: r=r_0/(1+\tau/\theta), with rotational correlation time \theta=\eta V/k_BT (~chemistrykit.photochem.perrin_anisotropy, rotational_correlation_time). A small dye depolarizes almost completely in water but stays polarized in viscous glycerol, and a &quot;Perrin plot&quot; of 1/r against T/\eta is a straight line whose intercept gives r_0 and whose slope gives the molecular volume.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/photochem/fluorescence/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_02_perrin_anisotropy_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/photochem/fluorescence/plot_02_perrin_anisotropy`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Perrin equation: fluorescence anisotropy and molecular rotation</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/photochem/fluorescence/plot_02_perrin_anisotropy`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Ernst Ising (1925) solved the chain of spins s_i=\pm1 with nearest-neighbor coupling J and found no spontaneous magnetization at any temperature above zero. Ising1D solves it with the 2x2 transfer matrix, whose eigenvalues \lambda_\pm give Z_N=\lambda_+^N+\lambda_-^N exactly. The plots show a checked partition function, a magnetization that is a smooth function of field at every temperature (it only becomes a step at T=0), a correlation length that grows as e^{2J/k_BT} but stays finite, and a smooth heat-capacity maximum instead of a singularity.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/statmech/ising/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_ising_chain_transfer_matrix_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/statmech/ising/plot_01_ising_chain_transfer_matrix`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Ising's one-dimensional chain: no phase transition</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/statmech/ising/plot_01_ising_chain_transfer_matrix`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Kramers and Wannier (1941) showed that the square-lattice Ising model&#x27;s high-temperature series at coupling K=J/k_BT equals its low-temperature series at the dual coupling K^*, with">

